    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are actually correct to question this, because the distinction between \"memorizing\" a specific technique and \"using\" a universal concept is often blurred and legally ambiguous. The line you are asking about is generally drawn at whether the specific knowledge represents a unique mentorship technique or generic programming skills; however, adding code to a new project that mirrors a proprietary logic learned from them could easily be deemed \"disclosure\" of the residual itself depending on how strictly the client interprets this.\n\nSince \"unaided memory\" is subjective, the Receiving Party bears the burden of proving what is truly generic versus what is specific to the client's system, making it a high-risk clause that frequently leads to disputes over intellectual property ownership. If the NDA treats everything learned as proprietary, then using it in your own work without explicit permission or the ability to prove it is your own original idea is a very risky strategy that should probably be explored with legal counsel first.\n\nUltimately, the safest interpretation is that this clause attempts to capture any unique methodology learned and freeze it in your memory as \"knowledge,\" preventing you from ever applying that specific variant to your own projects until you successfully separate it from the client's trade secrets.",
